1. Kubernetes (K8s) Orchestration & Architecture
Kubernetes and Docker are not the same; Kubernetes is used for orchestration. If a system can only handle 300 containers but you have 1,000 incoming, K8s simplifies the workload by distributing it across multiple computers in a cluster.  
Cluster Basics

A cluster is a collection of systems working together (e.g., 5 laptops where 1 is the Master and 4 are Worker nodes). AWS provides managed K8s via EKS.   Master Node Components
These are the core components that manage the cluster. If the master node crashes, the cluster fails.   ETC (etcd): Acts as the "brain" or main storage of the cluster.   Scheduler: Assigns workloads to available/free worker nodes.   Controllers: Includes Replica Set Controller and Job Controller, which assign tasks and continuously check the health of the nodes. 

Worker Node Components

These components run on the worker machines to execute the actual workloads.   

Pod: The smallest deployable unit, containing one or more containers. No matter how many containers are inside a single pod, they all share the same network and IP address.   

Container Runtime: Software like ContainerD (similar to Docker) used for actual containerization.   

Kubelet: The agent that communicates with the master node; all worker node updates happen through the Kubelet.   

Kube-Proxy: Handles networking, port exposing, and port forwarding. It is responsible for routing incoming traffic to the exact, correct pod.   

2. Terraform (Infrastructure as Code)Purpose: Terraform is a third-party Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool.  

 Multi-Cloud: It works across AWS, Azure, and GCP, which is highly useful because companies often do not rely on a single cloud provider.   
 
 Syntax: The scripting syntax remains consistent across different environments, utilizing .tf files (manifests).   Infrastructure 
 
 Setup: You must prepare network configurations in Terraform, including the VPC, Subnets, Internet Gateway, and Security Groups, before configuring the EC2 instance to use them.   
 
